{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 287 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d3407b5852738404b2d7f459bc1950acc7075ecb22649f2c0bd4762db1fb0226" :edb/db-before-t 286 :edb/db-after-t 287 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533599 50 #inst "2026-10-11T11:08:50.847Z" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186151834 1071 "If $x + 8 \\equiv 11\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,15)$, find an equivalent statement where $x$ is isolated." 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186151834 1071 "If $x + 8 \\equiv 11\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,15),$ find an equivalent statement where $x$ is isolated." 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186151834 1074 "The addition property of congruences states that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-c\\equiv b-c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nWe use the subtraction property to subtract $8$ from both sides of the given congruence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 8 & ≡11 & (mod15) \\\\ x + 8 - 8 & ≡11 - 8 & (mod15) \\\\ x & ≡3 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186151834 1074 "The addition property of congruences states that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-c\\equiv b-c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe use the subtraction property to subtract $8$ from both sides of the given congruence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+8 & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+8-8 & ≡11-8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186151836 1071 "Which of the following numbers is congruent to $0$ modulo $18$?\n\n1. $-45$\n2. $42$\n3. $-54$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186151836 1071 "Which of the following numbers is congruent to $0$ modulo $18?$\n\n- $-45$\n\n- $42$\n\n- $-54$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186151836 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n$, there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $18$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $18$.\n\nSo, since $18\\, \\mid \\,(-54)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n-54 \\equiv (-3) \\cdot {\\color{blue}18} \\equiv (-3)\\cdot 0\\equiv 0 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}18})\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $18$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186151836 1074 "First, recall that  \n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $18$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $18.$\n\nSo, since $18\\,|\\,(-54),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-54 \\equiv (-3) \\cdot {\\color{blue}18} \\equiv (-3)\\cdot 0\\equiv 0 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}18}).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $18.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186151839 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $x \\equiv 23\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$?\n\n1. $x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n2. $x \\equiv 3 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n3. $x \\equiv 5 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186151839 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $x \\equiv 23\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)?$ \n\n- $x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $x \\equiv 3 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $x \\equiv 5 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186151839 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n$. This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n$.\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that ${\\color{blue}6} \\cdot (-3) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}6})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\nx + 0\\begin{vmatrix}≡23 + 0 & (mod6) \\\\ x & ≡23 + 0\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡23 + 6 \\cdot (-3)\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡23 - 18\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡5 \\mid (mod6)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186151839 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  ${\\color{blue}6} \\cdot (-3) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}6})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡23+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x & ≡23+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡23+6\\cdot (-3)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡23-18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186151863 1074 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1896, and April 6th, 1896.\n\n- There were $2$ months with $31$ days (January and March).\n- There were $0$ months with $30$ days.\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February) since it was a leap year.\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and April 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\overbrace{31(2) + 30(0) + 29(1)}^{\\textrm{days to April 1st}} + 5\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n$31 \\equiv 3, \\qquad 30\\equiv 2, \\qquad 29\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 7)$.\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 2(31) + 0(30) + 1(29) + 5 \\\\\n&= ≡2(3) + 0(2) + 1(1) + 5\\begin{vmatrix}(mod7) \\\\ ≡6 + 0 + 1 + 5\\end{vmatrix}(mod7) \\\\\n&= ≡12\\begin{vmatrix}(mod7) \\\\ ≡5\\end{vmatrix}(mod7)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $5$ days after January 1st, 1896, namely Monday." 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186151863 1074 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1896, and April 6th, 1896. \n\n- There were $2$ months with $31$ days (January and March).\n\n- There were $0$ months with $30$ days.\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February) since it was a leap year.\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and April 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd \n= \\overbrace{31(2) + 30(0) + 29(1)}^{\\textrm{days to April 1st}} + 5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31 \\equiv 3, \\qquad 30\\equiv 2, \\qquad 29\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2(31)+0(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡2(3)+0(2)+1(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6+0+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $5$ days after January 1st, 1896, namely Monday." 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152482 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410708 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410709 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410711 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410714 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410724 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1039 "Simplifying Congruences" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152058 1039 "Using the Addition and Subtraction Properties" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410710 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410712 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410715 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410719 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152059 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410721 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152060 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410717 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152060 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410720 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152060 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410722 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152061 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410713 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152061 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410716 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152061 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410718 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152061 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410723 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152340 1050 600.0 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149383 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149387 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410703 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410704 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152473 1071 "Given that $a≡b(mod9)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152473 1071 "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152473 1074 "The subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can subtract the same integer from both sides of a congruence, keeping the modulus unchanged:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-2≡b-2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149460 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410695 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152481 1071 "You're given that January 1st, 2000, was a Saturday. Use congruences to determine the day of the week on November 4th, 2000.\n\n*Hint: The year 2000 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400$.*" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152481 1071 "You're given that January 1st, 2000, was a Saturday. Use congruences to determine the day of the week on November 4th, 2000.\n\n*Hint: The year 2000 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152481 1074 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2000, and November 1st, 2000. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 4th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+29(1)}}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(29)+3 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(1)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡14\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as January 1st, 2000, namely Saturday." 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149466 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410699 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152482 1071 "Given that $a≡b(mod13)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$25$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186152482 1071 "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186152482 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nThis gives rise to the following facts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,⟺\\,a≡b+kn\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ a≡b\\,⟺\\,a+kn≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can add a multiple of the modulus $13$ to one side of the congruence. Notice that \n\n$$\n-26=(-2)\\cdot 13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-26≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,13)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186153446 1064 "Recall that two integers $a$ and $b$ are *congruent modulo* $n$ if they have the same remainder when divided by $n$.\n\nMoreover, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $a-b$.\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a-b = kn\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nA convenient property of congruences is that we can add and subtract on both sides, just as with regular equations.\n\nConsider, for instance, the following true statement:\n\n$$\n22\\equiv 10 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nThe **addition property** allows us to add an integer to both sides. For example, adding $2$ to both sides gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}22 + 2 & ≡10 + 2 & (mod12) \\\\ 24 & ≡12 & (mod12)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is also a true statement since $24$ and $12$ both have a remainder of zero when divided by $12$.\n\nSimilarly, the **subtraction property** allows us to subtract an integer from both sides. For example, subtracting $9$ from both sides of our original statement gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}22 - 9 & ≡10 - 9 & (mod12) \\\\ 13 & ≡1 & (mod12)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $13$ and $1$ both have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $12$.\n\nIn general, the addition and subtraction properties state the following:\n\n$$\na≡b⟺a + c≡b + c(modn); a≡b⟺a - c≡b - c(modn)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186153446 1064 "Recall that two integers $a$ and $b$ are *congruent modulo* $n$ if they have the same remainder when divided by $n.$\n\nMoreover, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $a-b.$\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a-b = kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z.$ \n\nA convenient property of congruences is that we can add and subtract on both sides, just as with regular equations. \n\nConsider, for instance, the following true statement:\n\n$$\n22\\equiv 10 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nThe **addition property** allows us to add an integer to both sides. For example, adding $2$ to both sides gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22+2 & ≡10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 24 & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is also a true statement since $24$ and $12$ both have a remainder of zero when divided by $12.$\n\nSimilarly, the **subtraction property** allows us to subtract an integer from both sides. For example, subtracting $9$ from both sides of our original statement gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22-9 & ≡10-9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 13 & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $13$ and $1$ both have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $12.$\n\nIn general, the addition and subtraction properties state the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,⟺\\,a+c≡b+c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ a≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186153447 1064 "According to the definition of modular congruence, we have that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b)  \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = b+ kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Substituting $b=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid a  \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn\n$$\n\nThis gives rise to the following important fact:\n\n*Any integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $n.$*\n\nFor example, the numbers $10,$ $-25,$ and $75$ are all are all multiples of ${\\color{blue}5},$ so they are all congruent to $0$ modulo ${\\color{blue}5}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =2\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ -25 & =(-5)\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 75 & =15\\cdot 5≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186153447 1064 "According to the definition of modular congruence, we have that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid (a-b) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = b+ kn\n$$\n\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting $b=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad n \\mid a \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn\n$$\n\nThis gives rise to the following important fact:\n\n> *Any integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $n$.*\n\nFor example, the numbers $10$, $-25$, and $75$ are all are all multiples of ${\\color{blue}5}$, so they are all congruent to $0$ modulo ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}10 &= 2 \\cdot 5≡0 & (mod5) \\\\ -25|= (-5) \\cdot 5≡0 & (mod5) \\\\ 75|= 15 \\cdot 5≡0 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186153450 1064 "Let's try to simplify the right-hand side of the following congruence:\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 102 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nBy \"simplify,\" we mean that we wish to transform the number on the right-hand side to be the smallest possible positive integer. In other words, we want to write\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < 5\n$$\n\nWhen we add $0$ to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing $0$ as an integer multiple of $n$. This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:{\\color{black}n})$.\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that ${\\color{blue}5} \\cdot (-20) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}5})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡102 & (mod5) \\\\ x + 0 & ≡102 + 0 & (mod5) \\\\ x & ≡102 + 5 \\cdot (-20) & (mod5) \\\\ x & ≡102 - 100 & (mod5) \\\\ x & ≡2 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x \\equiv 102\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ is equivalent to $x \\equiv 2\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$.\n\nOur answer is correct because $102$ and $2$ both have a remainder of $2$ when divided by $5$, and\n\n$$\n0 \\leq 2 < 5\n$$\n\n**Note:** By the division algorithm, there is exactly one value of $r$ that satisfies\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < n\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186153450 1064 "Let's try to simplify the right-hand side of the following congruence:\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 102 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nBy \"simplify,\" we mean that we wish to transform the number on the right-hand side to be the smallest possible positive integer. In other words, we want to write\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < 5.\n$$\n\nWhen we add $0$ to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing $0$ as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to $0\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:{\\color{black}n}).$ \n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  ${\\color{blue}5} \\cdot (-20) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}5})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡102\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x+0 & ≡102+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & \\text{ }≡102+5\\cdot (-20)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & ≡102-100\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $x \\equiv 102\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)$ is equivalent to $x \\equiv 2\\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,5).$\n\nOur answer is correct because $102$ and $2$ both have a remainder of $2$ when divided by $5,$ and $0 \\leq  2 < 5.$\n\n**Note:** By the division algorithm, there is exactly one value of $r$ that satisfies\n\n$$\nx \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\\qquad\\textrm{for}\\qquad 0 \\leq r < n.\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186153472 1064 "In this lesson, we've been working with the so-called addition property of modular arithmetic.\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the addition property by writing $a\\equiv b$ in terms of divisibility:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟺\\,a-b=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a+c)-(b+c)=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a+c)≡(b+c)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe subtraction property is proved similarly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟺\\,a-b=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a-c)-(b-c)=kn \\\\  & ⟺\\,(a-c)≡(b-c)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186153472 1064 "In this lesson, we've been working with the so-called addition property of modular arithmetic.\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the addition property by writing $a\\equiv b$ in terms of divisibility:\n\n$$\na≡b(modn) \\mid ⟺a - b = kn; ⟺(a + c) - (b + c) = kn; ⟺(a + c)≡(b + c)(modn)\n$$\n\nThe subtraction property is proved similarly:\n\n$$\na≡b(modn) \\mid ⟺a - b = kn; ⟺(a - c) - (b - c) = kn; ⟺(a - c)≡(b - c)(modn)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 false] [17592186410580 1080 "Saturday" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410580 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410580 1083 #uuid "0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410581 1080 "22" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410581 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410581 1083 #uuid "09245c92-930e-5f0d-9d1f-efcbe7d45bf3" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410582 1080 "-84" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410582 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410582 1083 #uuid "0a659048-5bd4-5f99-9f41-4dcb9c78e3e6" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410583 1080 "2" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410583 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410583 1083 #uuid "0b852f2e-cc76-5abd-9729-0fa28782630f" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410584 1080 "3" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410584 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410584 1083 #uuid "0dd74d72-98fd-5356-b52c-46cdaa8786a3" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410585 1080 "50" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410585 1082