186410673 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410706 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410706 1077 "selection" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410706 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410636 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410706 1079 #uuid "f6519838-5e86-5791-bf74-fda374f69c7a" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410585 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410627 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410635 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410645 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1075 #edb/ref 17592186410667 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1077 "field-1" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1078 #edb/ref 17592186410645 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410707 1079 #uuid "f75f83ea-8131-580b-8fa9-3a2ff784fb28" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410693 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410698 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410708 1069 "q-212941" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410708 1071 "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410708 1072 #uuid "0ae69b79-17cb-5f18-82ec-b83f30862ca1" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410708 1074 "The subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can subtract the same integer from both sides of a congruence, keeping the modulus unchanged:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-6≡b-6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410684 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410692 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410709 1069 "q-212944" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410709 1071 "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410709 1072 #uuid "0b565593-fa14-5d8e-a413-f5f33c1ea21c" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410709 1074 "The subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can subtract the same integer from both sides of a congruence, keeping the modulus unchanged:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-5≡b-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410697 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410710 1069 "q-132085" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410710 1071 "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $15?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410710 1072 #uuid "1f9b7d3e-524b-551d-8a94-9d846396890c" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410710 1074 "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $15$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $15.$\n\nTherefore, since $15\\,|\\,60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n60≡4\\cdot 15≡4\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $15.$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410700 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410711 1069 "q-79667" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410711 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $x+5≡11\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410711 1072 #uuid "23919475-35c0-5151-a80d-eb2f21c5c2a9" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410711 1074 "The addition property of congruences states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a+c≡b+c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe use the subtraction property to subtract $5$ from both sides of the given congruence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+5-5 & ≡11-5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410702 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410712 1069 "q-79694" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410712 1071 "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $17?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410712 1072 #uuid "3911df9a-6841-59e6-b534-6e8a3aa2e9ba" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410712 1074 "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $17$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $17.$\n\nTherefore, since $17\\,|\\,(-51),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-51≡(-3)\\cdot 17≡(-3)\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,17).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $17.$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410687 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410713 1069 "q-213211" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410713 1071 "Jacopo Peri's Euridice, modern opera's earliest known, fully surviving work, was first performed in Florence on October 6th, 1600. Given that January 1st, 1600, was a Saturday, use congruences to determine the day of the week on which the opera was first performed.\n\n*Hint: The year 1600 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410713 1072 #uuid "50be2be7-4190-59bf-9880-ce7373f52df0" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410713 1074 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1600, and October 1st, 1600. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, and August).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and October 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to October 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(5)+30(3)+29(1)}}+5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,5≡5,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+3(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+3(2)+(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date 6 days after January 1st, 1600, namely Friday." 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410707 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410714 1069 "q-212951" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410714 1071 "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410714 1072 #uuid "5f92504f-2a88-577a-b7ee-007131f73594" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410714 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nThis gives rise to the following facts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,⟺\\,a≡b+kn\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\\\ a≡b\\,⟺\\,a+kn≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can add a multiple of the modulus $15$ to one side of the congruence. Notice that \n\n$$\n-45=(-3)\\cdot 15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-45≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410696 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410715 1069 "q-79692" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410715 1071 "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $14?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410715 1072 #uuid "6d54f83a-3712-59c6-b238-0e2bb4334321" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410715 1074 "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $14$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $14.$\n\nTherefore, since $14\\,|\\,(-84),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-84≡(-6)\\cdot 14≡(-6)\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $14.$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410690 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410716 1069 "q-213291" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410716 1071 "A parodic holiday, International Talk Like a Pirate Day, was celebrated for the first time on September 19th, 1995. Given that January 1st, 1995, was a Sunday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week this holiday was celebrated for the first time.\n\n*Hint: The year 1995 was not a leap year since it is not divisible by $4.$*" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410716 1072 #uuid "700ec10d-3e3a-58fc-aadf-2fee69932797" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410716 1074 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1995, and September 1st, 1995. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July,  and  August).\n\n- There were $2$ months with $30$ days (April and  June).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and September 19th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to September 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(5)+30(2)+28(1)}}+18.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,18≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+2(30)+1(28)+18 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+2(2)+1(0)+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $2$ days after January 1st, 1995, namely Tuesday." 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410685 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410717 1069 "q-79656" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410717 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡35\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410717 1072 #uuid "77cbdd5d-15bd-5f9e-950f-9243376a199b" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410717 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $11\\cdot (-3)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡35+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ x & ≡35+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡35+11\\cdot (-3)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡35-33\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410688 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410718 1069 "q-213292" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410718 1071 "On November 16th, 1700, Spain's King Charles II died. Given that January 1st, 1700, was a Friday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week Charles II died.\n\n*Hint: The year 1700 was not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and is not divisible by $400.$*" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410718 1072 #uuid "83693413-601d-53f0-b546-e5ed9a2ee614" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410718 1074 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1700, and November 1st, 1700. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 16th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+28(1)}}+15.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,15≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(28)+15 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(0)+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $4$ days after January 1st, 1700, namely Tuesday." 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410701 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410719 1069 "q-132087" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410719 1071 "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $16?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410719 1072 #uuid "abd575a7-0f26-5465-b126-ff96772a8759" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410719 1074 "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $16$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $16.$ \n\nTherefore, since $16\\,|\\,64,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n64≡4\\cdot 16≡4\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $16.$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410705 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410720 1069 "q-132097" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410720 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410720 1072 #uuid "bc99e19c-49e9-51e2-8ead-f8afe5651f31" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410720 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $7\\cdot 3≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ x & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+7\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+21\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡26\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410689 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410721 1069 "q-79693" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410721 1071 "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $4?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410721 1072 #uuid "c78b30a9-b100-5475-8a11-aa23c310f873" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410721 1074 "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4.$\n\nTherefore, since $4\\,|\\,52,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n52≡13\\cdot 4≡13\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $4.$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410706 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410722 1069 "q-132093" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410722 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)?$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410722 1072 #uuid "d19c623d-9750-5d15-989b-ed8e917e3481" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410722 1074 "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $12\\cdot (-2)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡26+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ x & ≡26+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡26+12\\cdot (-2)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡26-24\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410694 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410723 1069 "q-213293" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410723 1071 "Christmas in 2100 will be celebrated, as usual, on December 25th. Given that January 1st, 2100, is a Friday, use congruences to determine the day of the week on which Christmas will be celebrated in 2100.\n\n*Hint: The year 2100 is not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and is not divisible by $400.$*" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410723 1072 #uuid "f453d5f9-efe9-5d81-aae2-3ab414bd5f85" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410723 1074 "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2100, and December 1st, 2100. \n\n- There are $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There are $4$ months with $30$ days (April, June, September, and November).\n\n- There is $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and December 25th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to December 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(4)+28(1)}}+24.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,24≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+4(30)+1(28)+24 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+4(2)+(0)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+8+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+1+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $1$ day after January 1st, 2100, namely Saturday." 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410686 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410691 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410724 1069 "q-212938" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410724 1071 "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410724 1072 #uuid "f913a8a8-9a07-5f52-8a63-48e2094f05b2" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true] [17592186410724 1074 "The addition property of congruences states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a+c≡b+c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can add the same integer to both sides of a congruence, keeping the modulus unchanged:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a+1≡b+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)\n$$" 13194139533599 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" 17592186410580 "answer-09245c92-930e-5f0d-9d1f-efcbe7d45bf3" 17592186410581 "answer-0a659048-5bd4-5f99-9f41-4dcb9c78e3e6" 17592186410582 "answer-0b852f2e-cc76-5abd-9729-0fa28782630f" 17592186410583 "answer-0dd74d72-98fd-5356-b52c-46cdaa8786a3" 17592186410584 "answer-0ea93020-b73c-51c8-92b7-9b07d3bc82c8" 17592186410585 "answer-11771110-3806-54e3-a052-d7cb39b48fc0" 17592186410586 "answer-13926285-aa6b-5092-9d57-fa8e5980190e" 17592186410587 "answer-15c898bd-31e5-598a-9024-2b8ac1db97bd" 17592186410588 "answer-18713113-2b94-57e2-8357-e9ce34b2ae30" 17592186410589 "answer-1cf2b520-553b-53a7-88f5-8915ae76af14" 17592186410590 "answer-1d657fb4-9478-55fe-b9df-f986e75a7456" 17592186410591 "answer-1fa5f3a4-adf7-58c3-b075-de7d7b496c72" 17592186410592 "answer-226134f3-e697-5f75-9604-0da171ad901b" 175921